#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Linearizability checker for atomic range transactions.

A whole transaction is one atomic operation: the witness is a serial order of
the transactions (a linear extension of the "A.end <= B.start => A before B"
partial order) that, executed from the initial map, reproduces every
observation and the final map.  We must return such an order, or null when no
such order exists.

State model (identical to the reference semantics of a dict-backed store):
 * a key slot holds either ABSENT (key not present) or a value;
 * reading an absent key yields null, and a value may itself be null - the
   two cases read the same but are different slot contents, which matters
   for scans and for map equality;
 * "del" makes a slot ABSENT, a CAS whose new value is null deletes the key,
   a put stores its value verbatim.

Search strategy
---------------
* Bitmask DFS over "which transactions are already scheduled".  A search node
  is (mask, current map); nodes proven unsolvable are memoised, and so is the
  result of applying one transaction to a given map (the map, not the mask,
  decides the outcome).
* Cheap necessary-condition pruning:
    - a key whose slot still differs from its final slot needs a remaining
      transaction able to write exactly that slot value;
    - a pending read requirement of an unscheduled transaction needs a
      remaining possible producer (excluding a sole producer that needs the
      value itself).
* Transactions built only from puts and dels never read, so their
  applicability does not depend on the state and the problem reduces to
  choosing per-key last writers.  That case is solved by an exact backward
  greedy (see solve_write_only), which keeps write-only stress histories out
  of the subset search entirely.
"""

